WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.300
David: Welcome to LitShare.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.450
This is Protein Degradation English
Corner.

3
00:00:04.512 --> 00:00:05.762
Episode five.

4
00:00:07.825 --> 00:00:11.425
Angewandte Chemie International Edition,
Stephen B. H. Kent.

5
00:00:12.375 --> 00:00:16.725
Kinetically Controlled Ligation: Building
Proteins from Both Ends.

6
00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:20.650
This one is not a degradation story.

7
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.425
It is about building proteins, chemically,
from scratch.

8
00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:26.400
Here is the question.

9
00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:35.200
If you want to make a protein in the lab,
piece by piece, how do you join the pieces
together, in any order you like?

10
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.825
Alex: That is the question a team at the
University of Chicago set out to answer.

11
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.500
The paper is in Angewandte Chemie
International Edition, 2006.

12
00:00:46.925 --> 00:00:47.675
First author Duhee Bang.

13
00:00:49.275 --> 00:00:51.200
Corresponding author Stephen Kent.

14
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.825
Co-author Brad Pentelute.

15
00:00:54.475 --> 00:00:58.550
Their answer is a method called
kinetically controlled ligation.

16
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.900
K-C-L for short.

17
00:01:01.225 --> 00:01:02.600
David: Start with the background.

18
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.200
Chemical synthesis of proteins is not new,
right?

19
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:07.750
Alex: Right.

20
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:14.975
The standard method is native chemical
ligation, N-C-L. You take two peptide
segments.

21
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.925
One ends in a thioester, a sulfur-
containing reactive group.

22
00:01:20.325 --> 00:01:23.750
The other starts with the amino acid
cysteine.

23
00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:29.650
They react, and you get one longer
peptide, joined by a normal peptide bond.

24
00:01:30.275 --> 00:01:31.050
It works well.

25
00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:33.125
But it has a direction problem.

26
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.350
David: What is the direction problem?

27
00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:43.325
Alex: In native chemical ligation, you can
only build in one direction, toward the
N-terminus, the start of the chain.

28
00:01:44.275 --> 00:01:49.825
Each new segment must bring its own
thioester, and it must react before the
next one.

29
00:01:51.125 --> 00:01:59.200
You cannot protect the thioester group, so
you cannot hold a segment in reserve, and
you cannot build in the other direction.

30
00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:03.800
The synthesis is sequential, one segment
at a time.

31
00:02:04.125 --> 00:02:07.200
David: And that is slow, if the protein is
long.

32
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:08.450
Alex: Exactly.

33
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:15.875
A truly convergent synthesis would build
several pieces in parallel, then join them
all at the end.

34
00:02:16.475 --> 00:02:17.800
It is much more efficient.

35
00:02:18.325 --> 00:02:22.350
But without a way to protect the
thioester, you cannot do it.

36
00:02:23.125 --> 00:02:27.375
David: So what is the trick in kinetically
controlled ligation?

37
00:02:28.125 --> 00:02:29.650
Alex: The trick is a rate difference.

38
00:02:30.175 --> 00:02:31.900
There are two kinds of thioesters.

39
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:42.175
An aryl thioester, in this case a
thiophenyl ester, reacts with a cysteine
peptide much faster than a standard alkyl
thioester.

40
00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:50.975
In the same solution, without any added
thiophenol catalyst, the alkyl thioester
is effectively unreactive.

41
00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:52.600
It just sits there.

42
00:02:52.975 --> 00:02:55.225
David: So you can pick which one reacts.

43
00:02:56.025 --> 00:02:56.300
Alex: Yes.

44
00:02:56.925 --> 00:03:06.200
Take a bifunctional peptide: it starts
with cysteine at its N-terminus, and it
ends with an alkyl thioester at its
C-terminus.

45
00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:15.700
In a solution with an aryl thioester
partner and no added catalyst, it reacts
only through its N-terminal cysteine.

46
00:03:16.375 --> 00:03:21.625
Its own C-terminal thioester stays intact,
ready for the next step.

47
00:03:21.625 --> 00:03:23.825
David: That gives you the missing
direction.

48
00:03:24.625 --> 00:03:25.025
Alex: It does.

49
00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:31.525
Now you can build in the N-to-C direction,
toward the C-terminus, the end of the
chain.

50
00:03:32.225 --> 00:03:36.425
Combine that with the standard direction,
and you can build from both ends.

51
00:03:37.375 --> 00:03:39.975
David: There is also a protecting group in
this story.

52
00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:42.150
T-H-Z.

53
00:03:43.975 --> 00:03:46.500
Alex: Yes, T-H-Z, short for thiazolidine.

54
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.425
It is a masked form of cysteine.

55
00:03:50.975 --> 00:03:58.325
You can keep a cysteine protected as T-H-Z
while you do other chemistry, then unmask
it when you need it.

56
00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:08.425
And the unmasking is gentle: zero point
two molar methoxyamine hydrochloride, in
two hours, and the reaction is
quantitative.

57
00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.750
The thioester survives untouched.

58
00:04:12.275 --> 00:04:13.600
David: How gentle is gentle?

59
00:04:14.375 --> 00:04:16.500
The thioester is a reactive group.

60
00:04:17.075 --> 00:04:18.225
Does it really survive?

61
00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:20.750
Alex: That is the point of the paper.

62
00:04:21.525 --> 00:04:33.950
The methoxyamine treatment converts the
T-H-Z peptide to a cysteine peptide
quantitatively, and the alkyl thioester at
the other end is undamaged.

63
00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:57.300
You get a bifunctional building block,
ready to react at the cysteine end and
still carrying its thioester for later.

64
00:06:04.725 --> 00:06:05.725
David: Then the proof.

65
00:06:06.975 --> 00:06:08.775
They built a real protein.

66
00:06:09.825 --> 00:06:16.425
Alex: They built crambin, a small protein
of forty-six amino acids, from six peptide
segments.

67
00:06:16.975 --> 00:06:21.575
It is a model protein, well studied, with
three disulfide bonds.

68
00:06:22.275 --> 00:06:25.300
The plan is a good picture of convergent
synthesis.

69
00:06:26.125 --> 00:06:34.800
The left half, residues one to twenty-
five, is built toward the N-terminus, in
two kinetically controlled ligations.

70
00:06:35.375 --> 00:06:47.825
The right half, residues twenty-six to
forty-six, is built toward the C-terminus,
one kinetically controlled ligation plus
one standard native chemical ligation.

71
00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:50.250
David: Then the two halves join.

72
00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:52.225
Alex: In a final ligation, number five.

73
00:06:53.375 --> 00:06:58.825
The left half, carrying its alkyl
thioester, reacts with the right half,
which starts with cysteine twenty-six.

74
00:07:00.975 --> 00:07:04.250
Standard native chemical ligation
conditions, overnight.

75
00:07:04.875 --> 00:07:09.300
Then the full chain folds, and the three
disulfide bonds form.

76
00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:11.600
David: How well did the key step work?

77
00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.900
The first kinetically controlled ligation?

78
00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:16.250
Alex: Very well.

79
00:07:16.675 --> 00:07:24.675
At pH six point eight, with no thiophenol
added, it was complete in one hour, in
near-quantitative yield.

80
00:07:25.425 --> 00:07:33.875
The side products were small: less than
one point five percent of a cyclized
byproduct, and about one percent of
oligomer.

81
00:07:36.925 --> 00:07:37.225
David: And the folding?

82
00:07:37.225 --> 00:07:37.500
Alex: Also fast and clean.

83
00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:42.325
The full chain folded within one hour, in
near-quantitative yield.

84
00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:58.050
And the mass confirmed it: four thousand
seven hundred and two daltons observed,
against four thousand seven hundred and
two point four calculated, for the folded
protein with all three disulfide bonds
formed.

85
00:07:58.425 --> 00:07:59.825
David: So the chemistry works.

86
00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:01.375
What are the limits?

87
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:03.725
Alex: Three, mainly.

88
00:08:04.575 --> 00:08:09.100
First, this is one small model protein,
forty-six residues.

89
00:08:09.825 --> 00:08:23.525
Larger targets, erythropoietin, the H-I-V
one protease, and a potassium channel
interacting protein called K-C-H-I-P, were
in progress, but no results are shown
here.

90
00:08:23.975 --> 00:08:24.525
David: Second?

91
00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:28.050
Alex: The amino acid at the C-terminus
matters.

92
00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:30.825
A bulky residue slows the reaction.

93
00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:36.475
Isoleucine, threonine, and valine react
much more slowly than alanine.

94
00:08:37.375 --> 00:08:41.875
So when you design the segments, you have
to choose the cut points carefully.

95
00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:43.575
David: And the third?

96
00:08:44.225 --> 00:08:45.250
Alex: A side reaction.

97
00:08:45.925 --> 00:08:54.225
If a peptide has an internal cysteine, its
free thiol can attack the thioester and
form a thiolactone, a ring.

98
00:08:54.225 --> 00:08:59.950
The good news is that it converts back to
the alkyl thioester, so it is manageable.

99
00:09:00.625 --> 00:09:02.475
But it is something to watch for.

100
00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:04.200
David: Why does this matter?

101
00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:06.975
Forty-six residues is small.

102
00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:09.250
Alex: Because it proves the principle.

103
00:09:09.775 --> 00:09:14.075
Kinetically controlled ligation makes
fully convergent synthesis real.

104
00:09:14.725 --> 00:09:20.475
You can build both halves of a protein at
the same time, in parallel, and join them
at the end.

105
00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:23.650
For larger proteins, that changes the
arithmetic.

106
00:09:24.325 --> 00:09:35.950
And in the long run, chemical synthesis
can make proteins that biology cannot
easily make, with unnatural amino acids,
labels, or modifications built in from the
start.

107
00:09:36.275 --> 00:09:37.700
David: A method paper, then.

108
00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:42.000
Not a new protein, but a new way to build
proteins.

109
00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:43.350
Alex: Exactly.

110
00:09:44.325 --> 00:09:47.425
Native chemical ligation gave chemists one
direction.

111
00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:50.925
Kinetically controlled ligation gives them
the other.

112
00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:56.700
And with both directions, convergent
synthesis of proteins becomes possible.

113
00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.175
David: That is it for this episode.

114
00:09:59.675 --> 00:10:04.175
You have been listening to Protein
Degradation English Corner, from Litshare.

115
00:10:04.775 --> 00:10:05.700
See you next time.
